window.NOTEBOOK_CATALOG=[{"title": "机器人学基础", "path": "notes/robotics.html", "tags": ["运动学", "坐标变换"], "summary": "在三维空间中，旋转与平移共同描述刚体位姿。下面约定 $T_{AB}$ 将 B 系坐标转换到 A 系。", "body": "# 机器人学基础\n\n<span class=\"pill\">知识笔记 · 入门示例</span>\n\n标签：[运动学](../tags.md) · [坐标变换](../tags.md)\n\n## 刚体位姿\n\n在三维空间中，旋转与平移共同描述刚体位姿。下面约定 $T_{AB}$ 将 B 系坐标转换到 A 系。\n\n$$\nT_{AB}=\\begin{bmatrix}R_{AB}&t_{AB}\\\\0^\\top&1\\end{bmatrix}\\in SE(3)\n$$\n\n## 变换的组合\n\n$$ T_{AC}=T_{AB}T_{BC},\\qquad p_A=R_{AB}p_B+t_{AB} $$\n\n相乘顺序体现坐标系的连接关系；调试时首先检查上下标是否能够衔接。\n\n## 最小实现\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef transform_point(R, t, p):\n    \"\"\"R: (3,3), t/p: (3,)；将 B 系点转换至 A 系。\"\"\"\n    return R @ p + t\n\nR = np.eye(3)\nt = np.array([1., 0., 0.])\nassert np.allclose(transform_point(R, t, np.zeros(3)), t)\n```\n\n## 学习检查\n\n- 能解释主动旋转与坐标变换的区别。\n- 能通过单位矩阵和逆变换验证实现。\n- 下一步：推导差速运动学与雅可比矩阵。\n"}, {"title": "SLAM 与状态估计", "path": "notes/slam.html", "tags": ["SLAM", "概率估计"], "summary": "SLAM 联合估计机器人轨迹 $x_{0:T}$ 与地图 $m$。观测为 $z_{1:T}$，控制输入为 $u_{1:T}$。", "body": "# SLAM 与状态估计\n\n<span class=\"pill\">知识笔记 · 入门示例</span>\n\n标签：[SLAM](../tags.md) · [概率估计](../tags.md)\n\n## 问题定义\n\nSLAM 联合估计机器人轨迹 $x_{0:T}$ 与地图 $m$。观测为 $z_{1:T}$，控制输入为 $u_{1:T}$。\n\n$$ p(x_{0:T},m\\mid z_{1:T},u_{1:T}) $$\n\n## 从概率到优化\n\n在独立高斯残差假设下，最大后验估计可以写成加权最小二乘：\n\n```{math}\n:label: slam-objective\n\\mathbf{x}^{*}=\\arg\\min_{\\mathbf{x}}\\sum_i\n\\mathbf{r}_i(\\mathbf{x})^\\top\\Omega_i\\mathbf{r}_i(\\mathbf{x})\n```\n\n式 {eq}`slam-objective` 中 $\\Omega_i$ 为信息矩阵。异常匹配需要另外引入鲁棒核；固定参考位姿可以消除整体坐标系的规范自由度。\n\n## 系统拆解\n\n| 模块 | 输入 | 输出 | 检查项 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 前端 | 图像 / 点云 | 相对运动约束 | 关联质量 |\n| 后端 | 约束与初值 | 优化后的状态 | 残差与收敛 |\n| 回环 | 历史关键帧 | 非局部约束 | 误检率 |\n\n## 实验记录规范\n\n记录数据集、时间同步方式、标定版本与参数；比较轨迹前声明是否做尺度对齐。评价绝对轨迹误差时保留对齐方式和单位。\n"}, {"title": "运动规划", "path": "notes/planning.html", "tags": ["规划", "搜索"], "summary": "规划空间由自由空间 $\\mathcal C_{free}$ 和障碍空间组成。目标是在约束内连接起点与终点，而不是只找到视觉上平滑的曲线。", "body": "# 运动规划\n\n<span class=\"pill\">知识笔记 · 入门示例</span>\n\n标签：[规划](../tags.md) · [搜索](../tags.md)\n\n## 先明确问题\n\n规划空间由自由空间 $\\mathcal C_{free}$ 和障碍空间组成。目标是在约束内连接起点与终点，而不是只找到视觉上平滑的曲线。\n\n$$ \\gamma:[0,1]\\rightarrow\\mathcal C_{free} $$\n\n## A* 的评价函数\n\n$$ f(n)=g(n)+h(n) $$\n\n$g$ 是累计代价，$h$ 是剩余代价估计。图搜索在启发式一致等条件下可避免反复展开；实现时仍需处理更优路径更新。\n\n## 方法选择记录\n\n| 方法 | 适用场景 | 需要验证 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A* | 离散栅格 | 分辨率、邻域、启发式 |\n| RRT | 连续高维空间 | 碰撞检测、随机种子 |\n| 轨迹优化 | 已有可行初值 | 约束满足与局部极值 |\n\n## 实验清单\n\n固定地图和机器人外形；记录成功率、运行时间、路径长度与最小间隙。必须区分几何路径与满足速度、加速度约束的轨迹。\n"}, {"title": "控制理论", "path": "notes/control.html", "tags": ["控制", "PID"], "summary": "定义跟踪误差 $e(t)=r(t)-y(t)$。连续时间 PID 的基本形式为：", "body": "# 控制理论\n\n<span class=\"pill\">知识笔记 · 入门示例</span>\n\n标签：[控制](../tags.md) · [PID](../tags.md)\n\n## 从误差出发\n\n定义跟踪误差 $e(t)=r(t)-y(t)$。连续时间 PID 的基本形式为：\n\n$$ u(t)=K_p e(t)+K_i\\int_0^t e(\\tau)d\\tau+K_d\\frac{de(t)}{dt} $$\n\n## 离散实现要点\n\n```python\ndef pi_step(error, integral, dt, kp, ki, limit):\n    candidate = integral + error * dt\n    raw = kp * error + ki * candidate\n    output = max(-limit, min(limit, raw))\n    # 简单条件积分；工程使用前需验证边界行为\n    if abs(raw) <= limit or error * raw < 0:\n        integral = candidate\n    return output, integral\n```\n\n## 验证顺序\n\n1. 检查反馈符号、单位和采样周期。\n2. 在受限输入下验证阶跃响应。\n3. 记录超调、稳态误差和调节时间。\n4. 检查饱和、噪声和延迟下的行为。\n\n## 后续扩展\n\n建立状态空间模型，逐步加入 LQR 与 MPC 的推导和对比实验。\n"}, {"title": "机械结构设计", "path": "notes/mechanics.html", "tags": ["机械设计", "工程验证"], "summary": "结构笔记以负载、包络尺寸、速度、精度和制造约束为起点。所有参数都应注明单位、来源和安全系数的选取依据。", "body": "# 机械结构设计\n\n<span class=\"pill\">知识笔记 · 入门示例</span>\n\n标签：[机械设计](../tags.md) · [工程验证](../tags.md)\n\n## 从需求到结构\n\n结构笔记以负载、包络尺寸、速度、精度和制造约束为起点。所有参数都应注明单位、来源和安全系数的选取依据。\n\n## 传动选型的第一步\n\n理想情况下，功率与转矩、角速度的关系为：\n\n$$ P=\\tau\\omega $$\n\n加入传动效率 $\\eta$ 与减速比 $i=\\omega_{in}/\\omega_{out}$ 后：\n\n$$ \\tau_{out}=\\eta i\\tau_{in} $$\n\n## 设计评审表\n\n| 项目 | 记录内容 |\n| --- | --- |\n| 载荷 | 工况、冲击、持续时间 |\n| 刚度 | 允许位移与角偏差 |\n| 装配 | 基准、公差、维护空间 |\n| 验证 | 仿真边界条件与实测结果 |\n\n## 版本留痕\n\n每次结构改动同时记录 CAD 版本、变更原因、质量变化与待验证风险，避免只保存最终模型。\n"}, {"title": "专利分析", "path": "notes/patents.html", "tags": ["专利", "检索方法"], "summary": "这里用于记录技术方案与检索过程。示例不对应真实专利，也不包含法律状态结论。", "body": "# 专利分析\n\n<span class=\"pill\">知识笔记 · 入门示例</span>\n\n标签：[专利](../tags.md) · [检索方法](../tags.md)\n\n## 分析对象\n\n这里用于记录技术方案与检索过程。示例不对应真实专利，也不包含法律状态结论。\n\n## 阅读顺序\n\n先读摘要理解技术问题，再拆解独立权利要求中的技术要素，最后用附图和实施例解释部件关系。\n\n## 技术要素表\n\n| 要素编号 | 权利要求原文位置 | 技术作用 | 对应附图 | 待核实事项 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| A | 待填写 | 连接与定位 | 待填写 | 部件关系 |\n| B | 待填写 | 驱动与传动 | 待填写 | 约束条件 |\n\n## 检索日志\n\n保存检索日期、数据库、关键词、分类号、申请人和公开号。技术相似性、权利要求范围及法律状态分开记录；法律状态需要回到官方登记信息核验。\n\n## 输出模板\n\n使用 [专利分析模板](../templates.md)，把观察、原文证据和自己的推断分开书写。\n"}, {"title": "差速移动机器人导航", "path": "projects/mobile-robot.html", "tags": ["项目", "SLAM", "控制"], "summary": "", "body": "# 差速移动机器人导航\n\n<span class=\"pill\">示例项目 · 进行中</span>\n\n## 目标与范围\n\n在仿真中完成差速机器人建模、建图、定位与路径跟踪，保存一组可重现实验配置。此页面提供项目跟踪的起始结构。\n\n## 里程碑\n\n勾选项保存在当前浏览器；可导出记录，再同步回项目源文件。\n\n```{raw} html\n<div class=\"tracker\" data-project=\"mobile-robot\"><p><strong id=\"progress-label\">40% · 2 / 5 已完成</strong></p><progress id=\"progress\" max=\"5\" value=\"2\"></progress><label><input type=\"checkbox\" checked> 明确需求与仿真环境</label><label><input type=\"checkbox\" checked> 建立差速运动学模型</label><label><input type=\"checkbox\"> 完成地图构建与定位验证</label><label><input type=\"checkbox\"> 接入全局规划与路径跟踪</label><label><input type=\"checkbox\"> 记录实验指标与复盘</label><button id=\"export-progress\">导出进度 JSON</button><p class=\"muted\">仅本机保存 · 不会自动同步给其他设备</p></div>\n```\n\n## 核心模型\n\n轮半径为 $r$，左右轮间距为 $b$，角速度分别为 $\\omega_L,\\omega_R$：\n\n$$ v=\\frac r2(\\omega_R+\\omega_L),\\qquad \\omega=\\frac rb(\\omega_R-\\omega_L) $$\n\n## 验收与实验日志\n\n| 实验 | 指标 | 当前结果 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 直线跟踪 | 横向误差 RMS（m） | 待实验 |\n| 目标点导航 | 成功率、耗时 | 待实验 |\n| 避障 | 最小间隙（m） | 待实验 |\n\n## 关联知识\n\n[坐标变换](../notes/robotics.md) · [SLAM](../notes/slam.md) · [运动规划](../notes/planning.md) · [控制](../notes/control.md)\n"}, {"title": "把一次学习，变成可复用的知识", "path": "blog/first-note.html", "tags": ["学习记录"], "summary": "", "body": "# 把一次学习，变成可复用的知识\n\n2026-11-04 · 学习方法 · 3 分钟阅读 · 示例文章\n\n## 一个问题开始\n\n知识库不必从完整目录开始。先写下今天遇到的具体问题：一个坐标变换为何方向相反，或者一次轨迹跟踪为何振荡。\n\n## 三层记录\n\n第一层保留问题与现象，第二层给出推导和最小代码，第三层写清验证结果。只记结论，很容易丢掉结论适用的条件。\n\n## 一个可持续的节奏\n\n每次学习结束，用几分钟记录“理解了什么、证据是什么、接下来验证什么”。每周把重复出现的概念整理进知识库，把过程留在博客里。\n\n## 下一步\n\n用 [学习记录模板](../templates.md) 开始第一篇自己的记录，并将相关知识链接回对应项目。\n"}, {"title": "复用模板", "path": "templates.html", "tags": ["模板"], "summary": "", "body": "# 复用模板\n\n复制 MyST 模板，替换标题和内容后放入对应目录。每个模板均保留假设、证据和后续工作，方便持续修订。\n\n## 知识笔记\n\n````markdown\n# 主题名称\n标签：方向 · 方法\n## 问题与假设\n说明符号、单位和适用条件。\n## 推导\n```{math}\n:label: unique-equation-label\nx_{k+1}=Ax_k+Bu_k\n```\n## 最小实现\n```python\n# 可运行示例与输入输出\n```\n## 验证与边界\n## 参考资料\n````\n\n## 项目页面\n\n```markdown\n# 项目名称\n状态：计划中\n## 目标与验收标准\n## 里程碑\n- [ ] 需求与基线\n- [ ] 实现\n- [ ] 验证与复盘\n## 环境与复现步骤\n## 实验结果\n## 风险与下一步\n```\n\n## 专利分析\n\n```markdown\n# 专利名称 / 公开号\n检索日期：YYYY-MM-DD\n## 来源与基本信息\n## 技术问题\n## 独立权利要求拆解\n## 附图与实现方式\n## 证据与推断\n## 待核实信息\n```\n\n## 学习记录 / 博客\n\n```markdown\n# 本次学习的具体问题\n日期：YYYY-MM-DD\n标签：学习记录\n## 遇到的问题\n## 理解与证据\n## 最小实验\n## 尚未解决\n## 下一步\n```\n"}, {"title": "关于我", "path": "about.html", "tags": ["关于"], "summary": "", "body": "# 关于我\n\n## 研究兴趣\n\n这里是一个面向机器人系统的个人知识空间，关注机器人学、SLAM、运动规划、控制与机械结构设计，也记录专利阅读和持续学习。\n\n## 这个站点\n\n以可解释的推导、可运行的代码和可复现的实验为目标。示例内容用于展示组织方式，尚未填入个人履历和真实项目成果。\n\n## 待补充的个人信息\n\n姓名 / 简介、当前研究方向、代表项目、GitHub 与联系方式可在此页持续完善。\n\n## 内容约定\n\n引用外部资料时记录来源；实验结论说明条件；修订保留版本记录。\n"}, {"title": "维护指南", "path": "maintenance.html", "tags": ["维护"], "summary": "", "body": "# 维护指南\n\n## 本地构建\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npip install -r requirements.txt\npython scripts/build.py\n```\n\n构建结果位于 `dist/`。内容保存在 `docs/` 中，使用 MyST Markdown；主题为 Furo。公式由 MathJax 渲染，首次访问需要能连接其 CDN。\n\n## 新增与修订\n\n1. 从复用模板创建 `.md` 文件。\n2. 在对应栏目页面的 `toctree` 中加入文件路径。\n3. 在 `catalog.json` 中登记标题、路径、标签和摘要。\n4. 执行构建并检查链接、公式和移动端布局。\n5. 提交源码；项目里程碑的浏览器记录需手动同步回源文件。\n\n## GitHub Pages\n\n源码已提供 `.github/workflows/pages.yml`。将源码推送到你自己的 GitHub 仓库，在 Settings → Pages 中选择 GitHub Actions。工作流构建并上传 `dist/`。\n\n当前站点通过 Sites 发布；尚未创建或绑定 GitHub 仓库。\n\n## 目录结构\n\n```text\ndocs/\n  notes/       # 稳定知识\n  projects/    # 项目与实验\n  blog/        # 日期型记录\n  _static/     # 样式与交互\ncatalog.json   # 分类、标签与检索目录\nscripts/build.py\n.github/workflows/pages.yml\n```\n\n## 技术文档\n\n[Sphinx](https://www.sphinx-doc.org/) · [MyST 数学语法](https://myst-parser.readthedocs.io/en/latest/syntax/math.html) · [Furo 主题](https://furo.readthedocs.io/)\n"}, {"title": "知识库", "path": "knowledge.html", "tags": ["知识库"], "summary": "", "body": "# 知识库\n\n## Course Topics\n\n```{toctree}\n:maxdepth: 1\n\ncourses/index\n```\n\n## 研究方向\n\n- [机器人学基础](notes/robotics.md) — 运动学,坐标变换\n- [SLAM 与状态估计](notes/slam.md) — SLAM,概率估计\n- [运动规划](notes/planning.md) — 规划,搜索\n- [控制理论](notes/control.md) — 控制,PID\n- [机械结构设计](notes/mechanics.md) — 机械设计,工程验证\n- [专利分析](notes/patents.md) — 专利,检索方法\n\n## 阅读路径\n\n机器人学基础 → 状态估计 → 运动规划 → 控制 → 系统实验。机械设计与专利分析作为并行研究方向。\n\n## 分类约定\n\n每篇笔记属于一个主要方向，使用 2–4 个具体标签。概念推导进入知识库，可复现实验进入项目，带日期的观察进入博客。\n"}, {"title": "项目", "path": "projects.html", "tags": ["项目"], "summary": "", "body": "# 项目\n\n让每个项目都有可检验的目标、清晰的里程碑和可复现的实验。\n\n```{toctree}\n:maxdepth: 1\n\nprojects/mobile-robot\n```\n\n## 项目约定\n\n状态统一为：计划中 → 进行中 → 验证中 → 已完成。进度由已完成里程碑数量计算，避免主观估算。\n\n新项目可从 [项目模板](templates.md) 开始。当前展示的导航项目为示例，不代表个人真实研究成果。\n"}, {"title": "博客与学习记录", "path": "blog.html", "tags": ["学习记录"], "summary": "", "body": "# 博客与学习记录\n\n记录问题出现的上下文，保留从“不理解”到“能解释”的过程。\n\n```{toctree}\n:maxdepth: 1\n\nblog/first-note\n```\n\n## 归档\n\n2026 年 11 月 · 1 篇初始示例。新增文章使用日期与主题命名，并加入本页目录。\n"}, {"title": "Mathematics for Robotics", "path": "courses/robotics-math/index.html", "tags": ["Preliminary Mathematics for Robotics", "Mathematics for Robotics"], "summary": "These notes cover proofs, linear algebra, and inner product spaces, then use those tools to solve least squares problems, estimate states, and establish when an optimum exists.", "body": "# Mathematics for Robotics\n\nThese notes cover proofs, linear algebra, and inner product spaces, then use those tools to solve least squares problems, estimate states, and establish when an optimum exists.\n\n```{toctree}\n:maxdepth: 1\n\nch1\nch2\nch3\nch4\nch5\nch6\nch7\n```\n\n**Credit**\n\nBased on *Mathematics for Robotics* course notes by Jessy Grizzle, University of Michigan.\n"}, {"title": "Linear System Theory", "path": "courses/linear-systems.html", "tags": ["Preliminary Mathematics for Robotics", "Linear System Theory"], "summary": "Lecture notes are pending.", "body": "# Linear System Theory\n\nLecture notes are pending.\n"}, {"title": "Chapter 1. Mathematical Arguments", "path": "courses/robotics-math/ch1.html", "tags": ["Preliminary Mathematics for Robotics", "Mathematics for Robotics"], "summary": "Quantifier order, implications, proof techniques, truth tables, and the distinction between bounds and attained extrema.", "body": "# Chapter 1. Mathematical Arguments\n\nSuppose every run of a robot has a bounded tracking error. Does one bound work for all runs? The answer depends on who gets to choose the bound, and when. Quantifiers express that dependence. Proofs establish whether a proposed guarantee follows from its assumptions.\n\n## 1.1 Notation and Quantifiers\n\nThe domain determines which values a variable may take. Here $\\mathbb N=\\{1,2,\\ldots\\}$ denotes the counting numbers, $\\mathbb Z$ the integers, $\\mathbb Q$ the ratios of integers with nonzero denominator, $\\mathbb R$ the reals, and $\\mathbb C$ the complex numbers. The expression $x\\in A$ restricts $x$ to the set $A$.\n\nRead $\\forall$ as “for every” and $\\exists$ as “there exists.” For a universal claim, the proof must work for an arbitrary element of the domain. For an existence claim, one valid choice, called a *witness*, suffices.\n\n**Example: a bound for each run.** Let $e_k$ be the peak tracking error on run $k$. Compare the two guarantees below.\n\n| Guarantee | When is the bound chosen? |\n|---|---|\n| $\\forall k\\in\\mathbb N,\\ \\exists B\\in\\mathbb R,\\ e_k\\le B$ | After seeing the run index. Each run may have its own bound. |\n| $\\exists B\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\forall k\\in\\mathbb N,\\ e_k\\le B$ | Once, before choosing the run. The same bound must work for every run. |\n\nSet $e_k=k$. For the first guarantee, choose $B=k$. For the second, take any proposed finite $B$ and choose an integer $k>B$. This run violates the bound. Switching the quantifiers has changed what the robot is claimed to guarantee.\n\n## 1.2 Implications, Converses, and Proof Vocabulary\n\nIn an implication $P\\Rightarrow Q$, $P$ is sufficient for $Q$, and $Q$ is necessary for $P$. Its **contrapositive**, $\\neg Q\\Rightarrow\\neg P$, expresses the same implication. Its **converse**, $Q\\Rightarrow P$, requires a separate argument. For example, divisibility by four implies evenness. The integer 6 disproves the converse. To establish $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$, prove both directions.\n\n| Term | Role in an argument |\n|---|---|\n| Definition | Gives a precise meaning. For example, an integer is even exactly when it equals $2k$ for some integer $k$. |\n| Axiom | Supplies a starting assumption of the mathematical system. |\n| Theorem or proposition | States a result established by proof. |\n| Lemma | Establishes an intermediate result that another proof can use. |\n| Claim | Identifies an assertion to justify, often within a longer proof. |\n| Corollary | Follows from an established result with a short deduction. |\n| Conjecture | States a proposed result whose proof is still open in the discussion. |\n\n## 1.3 Proof Techniques\n\n**Direct proof.** To prove that the sum of two odd integers is even, write them as $2a+1$ and $2b+1$. Their sum is $2(a+b+1)$. The integer $a+b+1$ is the witness required by the definition of evenness.\n\n**Contraposition.** Suppose the goal is to show that an integer with an even square must itself be even. Start with an odd integer $n=2k+1$. Its square is $2(2k^2+2k)+1$, which is odd. This proves the contrapositive and hence the claim about even squares.\n\n**Exhaustion.** To show that $n(n+1)$ is even for every integer $n$, split the domain into two cases. If $n$ is even, the first factor is even. If $n$ is odd, the second factor is even. These cases cover every integer.\n\n**Induction.** To show that the first $n$ positive odd integers sum to $n^2$, check the first case: $1=1^2$. Assume the sum through the $k$th term is $k^2$. Adding the next odd integer gives $k^2+(2k+1)=(k+1)^2$. The base case and the step together establish the formula for every $n\\ge1$.\n\n**Strong induction.** Every integer $n\\ge2$ is a product of primes. The base case is 2. Assume the factorization exists for every integer from 2 through $k$. If $k+1$ is prime, it supplies its own factorization. Otherwise $k+1=ab$, where both factors lie between 2 and $k$. Apply the hypothesis to each factor and concatenate the products. Strong induction is convenient because the proof needs two earlier indices. Ordinary induction gives the same reasoning if its induction statement collects *all* cases through $k$.\n\n**Contradiction.** Suppose $\\sqrt 2=a/b$ in lowest terms, with $b>0$. Then $a^2=2b^2$, so the even-square result above makes $a$ even. Substituting $a=2k$ gives $b^2=2k^2$, so $b$ is even as well. The common factor 2 contradicts the choice of lowest terms. Therefore $\\sqrt 2$ is irrational.\n\n## 1.4 Truth Tables\n\nThe operators $\\neg$, $\\land$, and $\\lor$ mean negation, conjunction, and inclusive disjunction. Compare the last two columns to see exactly when an implication fails.\n\n| $P$ | $Q$ | $P\\land Q$ | $P\\lor Q$ | $P\\Rightarrow Q$ | $P\\land\\neg Q$ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| T | T | T | T | T | F |\n| T | F | F | T | F | T |\n| F | T | F | T | T | F |\n| F | F | F | F | T | F |\n\nA counterexample to $P\\Rightarrow Q$ must satisfy the hypothesis and violate the conclusion. This explains why a contradiction proof of an implication begins by assuming $P\\land\\neg Q$.\n\n## 1.5 Negating Logical Statements\n\nMove the negation inward one operation at a time. Negating “every” gives “some,” and negating “some” gives “every.” Keep the original domains. At the innermost condition, the negation of $a<b$ is $a\\ge b$, including equality.\n\nFor the uniform tracking guarantee in §1.1, this gives\n\n$$\n\\neg\\big(\\exists B\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\forall k\\in\\mathbb N,\\ e_k\\le B\\big)\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\forall B\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\exists k\\in\\mathbb N,\\ e_k>B.\n$$\n\n**Worked example: failure of continuity.** For a real-valued function on $\\mathbb R$, continuity at $x_0$ means that for every tolerance $\\varepsilon>0$, some radius $\\delta>0$ works for every input within that radius. Negating this statement gives\n\n$$\n\\exists\\varepsilon>0,\\ \\forall\\delta>0,\\ \\exists x\\in\\mathbb R:\n\\quad |x-x_0|<\\delta\n\\quad\\land\\quad |f(x)-f(x_0)|\\ge\\varepsilon.\n$$\n\nOne fixed output tolerance defeats every proposed input radius. The input must be *inside* the proposed neighborhood: this condition survives because negating the original implication requires its hypothesis to hold.\n\n## 1.6 Bounds, Extrema, and Completeness\n\nLet $A$ be a nonempty subset of $\\mathbb R$. An **upper bound** $u$ satisfies $a\\le u$ for every $a\\in A$. The **supremum** is the least such bound. A **maximum** is an upper bound belonging to $A$. Reversing the inequalities gives a lower bound, the **infimum** (greatest lower bound), and a **minimum** belonging to $A$.\n\n**Why does $(0,1)$ have supremum 1?** First, every member is at most 1. Second, take any proposed smaller upper bound $u<1$. The midpoint between $\\max\\{0,u\\}$ and 1 belongs to $(0,1)$ and exceeds $u$. This rules out every smaller candidate. The interval has no maximum because 1 lies outside it. Closing the interval at the right endpoint gives $(0,1]$ the same supremum and a maximum of 1.\n\n**Completeness of the reals.** Every nonempty subset of $\\mathbb R$ bounded above has a real supremum. The corresponding statement holds for infima of sets bounded below. The choice of number system matters: the positive rationals whose squares are less than 2 approach $\\sqrt 2$ from below. Their supremum in $\\mathbb R$ is $\\sqrt 2$. Within $\\mathbb Q$, every rational upper bound is strictly larger, and there is another rational number between it and $\\sqrt 2$. Thus no rational upper bound is the least one.\n\nFor an unbounded set, the extended-real conventions are $\\sup A=+\\infty$ when $A$ is unbounded above and $\\inf A=-\\infty$ when it is unbounded below. These symbols describe unboundedness. A robot whose peak errors satisfy $e_k=k$ attains a finite peak on every run, yet the supremum over all runs is $+\\infty$.\n"}, {"title": "Chapter 2. Abstract Linear Algebra", "path": "courses/robotics-math/ch2.html", "tags": ["Preliminary Mathematics for Robotics", "Mathematics for Robotics"], "summary": "Use a polynomial trajectory to work through bases, coordinate changes, eigenvectors, and rank.", "body": "# Chapter 2. Abstract Linear Algebra\n\nA robot's reference trajectory can be a vector in a space of functions. Choosing a basis turns that trajectory into a list of coefficients. Which properties belong to the trajectory, and which depend on the list? We will use a short polynomial trajectory to track the distinction.\n\n## 2.1 Fields and Vector Spaces\n\nA **field** supplies scalars with addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division by any nonzero element. The real and complex numbers are fields. The integers fail the division test: the equation $2a=1$ has no integer solution.\n\nA **vector space** over a field is closed under addition and scalar multiplication, with a zero vector, additive inverses, and the usual associative and distributive laws. For example, let $\\mathcal P_2$ contain the real polynomials of degree at most two, including zero. Adding two such trajectories or scaling one keeps the result in $\\mathcal P_2$. Polynomials of *exactly* degree two fail this test because their leading terms can cancel.\n\n## 2.2 Subspaces\n\nA nonempty subset $W$ of a vector space is a subspace precisely when $\\alpha u+\\beta v\\in W$ for all $u,v\\in W$ and all scalars $\\alpha,\\beta$. The remaining vector-space laws are inherited from the ambient space.\n\n**Test a trajectory constraint.** The constraint $p(0)=0$ defines a subspace of $\\mathcal P_2$: evaluating a linear combination at zero gives $\\alpha p(0)+\\beta q(0)=0$. The constraint $p(0)=1$ defines a translate of that subspace. It excludes the zero trajectory. In matrix language, this is the distinction between the kernel $Ax=0$ and the feasible set $Ax=b$ with $b\\ne0$.\n\n## 2.3 Linear Combinations and Independence\n\nThe **span** of a set contains every finite linear combination of its elements. Vectors are independent when $\\sum_i a_iv_i=0$ forces each $a_i=0$. A dependence relation with $a_j\\ne0$ expresses $v_j$ as a combination of the remaining vectors, so removing it preserves the span.\n\nFor polynomials, $a+bt+ct^2=0$ as a function means the equality holds at every $t$. Evaluating at $0,1,-1$ gives $a=0$, $b+c=0$, and $-b+c=0$, forcing all three coefficients to vanish. The choice of field matters too: $1,i$ are independent over the reals, while complex scalars permit $i\\cdot1-1\\cdot i=0$.\n\n## 2.4 Bases and Dimension\n\nAn ordered **basis** spans the space and is linearly independent. Spanning gives existence of coordinates. Independence gives uniqueness: subtract two representations and the resulting zero combination forces the coefficient lists to agree.\n\nThe ordered basis $(1,t,t^2)$ makes $\\dim\\mathcal P_2=3$. In a space of dimension $n$, any $n$ independent vectors form a basis. To extend a shorter independent list, add a vector outside its span and repeat. All polynomials of unrestricted degree form an infinite-dimensional space because the list of independent monomials can grow without bound.\n\n## 2.5 Coordinates and Change of Basis\n\nLet $E=(1,t,t^2)$ and $B=(1,t,1+t^2)$. Write the columns of $S$ as the coordinates of the $B$ basis vectors in $E$. For $p(t)=2-t+3t^2$ this gives\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&1\\\\0&1&0\\\\0&0&1\\end{bmatrix},\n\\qquad [p]_E=S[p]_B,\\qquad\n[p]_E=(2,-1,3)^T,\\quad [p]_B=(-1,-1,3)^T .\n$$\n\nCheck the direction by reconstructing $p=-1-t+3(1+t^2)$. To convert from $E$ to $B$, solve $Sc=[p]_E$. Reversing the direction of the basis change is a common source of coordinate errors.\n\n## 2.6 Linear Operators and Matrix Representations\n\nA map $L$ is linear if $L(\\alpha u+\\beta v)=\\alpha L(u)+\\beta L(v)$. Its action on a basis determines its action everywhere. For differentiation on $\\mathcal P_2$, the images of $1,t,t^2$ are $0,1,2t$. Placing their coordinates in columns gives\n\n$$\nD_E=\\begin{bmatrix}0&1&0\\\\0&0&2\\\\0&0&0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by $(2,-1,3)^T$ returns $(-1,6,0)^T$, the coefficients of the velocity $-1+6t$. In the other basis the same operator has matrix $D_B=S^{-1}D_ES$: convert input coordinates to $E$, differentiate, then convert the output back.\n\n## 2.7 Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, and Diagonalization\n\nAn eigenpair satisfies $Av=\\lambda v$ with $v\\ne0$. The characteristic equation $\\det(\\lambda I-A)=0$ supplies candidate eigenvalues. For each one, solve $(A-\\lambda I)v=0$ to find its eigenspace. If $V$ contains a basis of eigenvectors, $AV=V\\Lambda$ and $A^k=V\\Lambda^kV^{-1}$. Powers then reduce to scalar powers along the eigenvectors.\n\nDistinct eigenvalues give independent eigenvectors. A repeated eigenvalue requires checking the eigenspace dimension. The differentiation matrix above is triangular with three zero eigenvalues, but only constant polynomials have zero derivative. Its eigenspace has dimension one, so no eigenvector basis exists.\n\n## 2.8 Rank and Null Spaces\n\nThe rank is the dimension of the image. For $A$ with $n$ columns, $\\operatorname{rank}A+\\dim\\ker A=n$. An observation $b$ can be explained exactly when $b$ belongs to the image. Given one solution $x_0$, every solution has the form $x_0+z$ with $Az=0$. For differentiation, rank two and nullity one explain why a velocity determines a quadratic trajectory only up to an additive constant.\n\nFor real $A$, $x^TA^TAx=\\|Ax\\|_2^2$, so $A^TA$ and $A$ have the same kernel. Thus $A^TA$ is invertible exactly when the columns of $A$ are independent. This is the rank test behind the [least squares normal equations](ch3.md).\n\n**A reusable matrix identity.** With invertible $M$ and $1+v^TM^{-1}u\\ne0$, a rank-one change has inverse\n$$\n(M+uv^T)^{-1}=M^{-1}-\\frac{M^{-1}uv^TM^{-1}}{1+v^TM^{-1}u}.\n$$\nMultiplying by $M+uv^T$ cancels the correction terms. When one sensor observation adds a single outer product to a normal matrix, this identity updates the inverse using the old one.\n"}, {"title": "Chapter 3. Inner Product Spaces and Least Squares", "path": "courses/robotics-math/ch3.html", "tags": ["Preliminary Mathematics for Robotics", "Mathematics for Robotics"], "summary": "Derive normal equations, weighted fits, minimum-norm solutions, and recursive least squares from projection.", "body": "# Chapter 3. Inner Product Spaces and Least Squares\n\nTwo measurements of the same distance read 2 and 4. A model that predicts $(x,x)$ cannot match both. Choosing how to measure the residual turns this inconsistent system into a well-defined approximation problem.\n\n## 3.1 Norms and Normed Spaces\n\nA norm is nonnegative, vanishes only at zero, satisfies $\\|\\alpha x\\|=|\\alpha|\\|x\\|$, and obeys the triangle inequality. For a residual $r$, the one-norm sums absolute errors, the two-norm measures Euclidean distance, and the maximum norm reports the largest absolute error.\n\nWith readings 2 and 4, minimizing $|x-2|+|x-4|$ gives every $x$ in $[2,4]$: the sum is exactly 2 throughout. Minimizing the sum of squares gives a unique estimate, 3. The choice of norm can change both the estimate and its uniqueness.\n\n## 3.2 Inner Products\n\nA real inner product is symmetric, bilinear, and positive definite. Over complex scalars, conjugate symmetry and conjugation in one argument replace real symmetry and bilinearity. The induced norm is $\\|u\\|=\\sqrt{\\langle u,u\\rangle}$. Cauchy–Schwarz gives $|\\langle u,v\\rangle|\\le\\|u\\|\\|v\\|$. When $\\langle u,v\\rangle=0$, expanding the square gives $\\|u+v\\|^2=\\|u\\|^2+\\|v\\|^2$.\n\nFor continuous real trajectories on $[0,1]$, use $\\langle f,g\\rangle=\\int_0^1 f(t)g(t)\\,dt$. The functions $1$ and $t-1/2$ are orthogonal because the positive and negative signed areas of $t-1/2$ cancel.\n\n## 3.3 Gram–Schmidt Orthogonalization\n\nGiven linearly independent real vectors $a_1,\\dots,a_n$, construct the next direction by removing its components along the orthonormal directions already found:\n\n$$\nu_j=a_j-\\sum_{i<j}\\langle q_i,a_j\\rangle q_i,\\qquad q_j=u_j/\\|u_j\\|.\n$$\n\nFor $a_1=1$ and $a_2=t$ with the integral inner product, $q_1=1$, $\\langle 1,t\\rangle=1/2$, and $\\|t-1/2\\|^2=1/12$. The second unit direction is $\\sqrt{12}(t-1/2)$. Both bases span the same affine functions of time.\n\nAn exactly zero remainder signals dependence. In floating-point arithmetic near-dependent inputs can lose orthogonality. Modified Gram–Schmidt projects the progressively updated remainder onto each successive direction.\n\n## 3.4 The Projection Theorem and Normal Equations\n\nFor a finite-dimensional subspace $M$, a vector $p\\in M$ is the closest point to $b$ exactly when $b-p$ is orthogonal to $M$. To see sufficiency, take any $m\\in M$. Orthogonality gives $\\|b-m\\|^2=\\|b-p\\|^2+\\|p-m\\|^2$. For necessity, move a candidate minimizer in any direction $v\\in M$ and differentiate $\\|b-p-tv\\|^2$ at $t=0$.\n\nFor $M=\\operatorname{col}A$, this condition becomes $A^T A\\hat x=A^Tb$. In the distance example the model column is $(1,1)^T$ and $b=(2,4)^T$. The normal equation is $2\\hat x=6$. The residual $(-1,1)^T$ is orthogonal to the model column, directly verifying optimality. Dependent columns still give a unique projected vector $A\\hat x$, even when several parameter vectors produce it.\n\n## 3.5 Symmetric and Orthogonal Matrices\n\nThe spectral theorem gives $H=U\\Lambda U^T$ for real symmetric $H$, where $U^TU=I$ and $\\Lambda$ contains real eigenvalues. Eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal: if $Hv=\\lambda v$ and $Hw=\\mu w$, symmetry equates $v^THw$ with $(Hv)^Tw$, giving $(\\mu-\\lambda)v^Tw=0$. Inside a repeated eigenspace one can choose an orthonormal basis.\n\nAn orthogonal coordinate change preserves lengths because $\\|Uz\\|^2=z^TU^TU z$. In these coordinates a quadratic cost $x^THx$ separates into $\\sum_i\\lambda_i z_i^2$, where $z=U^Tx$.\n\n## 3.6 Quadratic Forms, Positive Definiteness, and Schur Complements\n\nA symmetric matrix is positive definite if $x^THx>0$ for every nonzero $x$, equivalently if all its eigenvalues are positive. Positive semidefiniteness allows zero eigenvalues. For a symmetric block matrix with top-left block $A\\succ0$, completing the square gives\n\n$$\nx^TAx+2x^TBy+y^TCy\n=(x+A^{-1}By)^TA(x+A^{-1}By)+y^T(C-B^TA^{-1}B)y.\n$$\n\nThe full matrix is positive definite precisely when its **Schur complement** $C-B^TA^{-1}B$ is positive definite. For $\\left[\\begin{smallmatrix}4&2\\\\2&c\\end{smallmatrix}\\right]$, the threshold is $c>1$. At $c=1$ the cost equals $(2x+y)^2$, which vanishes along an entire line.\n\n## 3.7 Least Squares Problems\n\n**Weighted observations.** Minimize $(b-Ax)^TW(b-Ax)$ with $W\\succ0$. Orthogonality in this weighted inner product gives $A^TWA\\hat x=A^TWb$. For the two distance readings, weights 1 and $1/4$ give $\\hat x=(2+4/4)/(1+1/4)=2.4$. The first reading now carries four times the weight of the second.\n\n**An exact constraint with many solutions.** For full-row-rank $A$, minimize $x^TWx$ subject to $Ax=b$. Stationarity gives $Wx=A^T\\lambda$ after scaling the multiplier. Substitution gives $x=W^{-1}A^T(AW^{-1}A^T)^{-1}b$. With $W=I$ and $x_1+x_2=6$, the minimum-norm allocation is $(3,3)$.\n\n**Recursive least squares.** Once the accumulated normal matrix $H$ is invertible, keep $P=H^{-1}$ and the current estimate. For one new scalar reading $y$ with model row $a^T$ and weight $w>0$, the new normal matrix is $H+waa^T$. Applying the rank-one inverse identity gives\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{Pa}{w^{-1}+a^TPa},\\qquad\n\\hat x_{\\rm new}=\\hat x+K(y-a^T\\hat x),\\qquad\nP_{\\rm new}=P-Ka^TP .\n$$\n\nThe scalar in parentheses is the discrepancy of the new measurement. Starting from the single reading 2 with unit weight gives $P=1$. Adding 4 with weight $1/4$ gives $K=1/5$ and the same estimate 2.4 as the batch calculation.\n"}, {"title": "Chapter 4. Matrix Factorizations", "path": "courses/robotics-math/ch4.html", "tags": ["Preliminary Mathematics for Robotics", "Mathematics for Robotics"], "summary": "Choose between QR, SVD, pivoted LU, and Cholesky using worked linear systems and a weakly observed parameter.", "body": "# Chapter 4. Matrix Factorizations\n\nA matrix factorization rewrites one difficult operation as several simpler ones. QR uses orthonormal coordinates to solve a fit. SVD exposes poorly measured directions. LU and Cholesky turn repeated square solves into triangular substitutions.\n\n## 4.1 QR Factorization\n\nFor a real $m\\times n$ matrix with independent columns and $m\\ge n$, reduced QR gives $A=QR$, with $Q^TQ=I_n$ and $R$ upper triangular. In Gram–Schmidt, the entries of column $j$ of $R$ are the coefficients of $a_j$ along $q_1,\\ldots,q_j$; the diagonal entry is the norm of the remainder before normalization.\n\nThe squared residual splits into perpendicular pieces:\n\n$$\n\\|Ax-b\\|_2^2=\\|Rx-Q^Tb\\|_2^2+\\|(I-QQ^T)b\\|_2^2 .\n$$\n\nOnly the first term depends on $x$. Solve $Rx=Q^Tb$ by back substitution. For $A=(1,1)^T$, $Q=(1,1)^T/\\sqrt2$ and $R=\\sqrt2$. With $b=(2,4)^T$, the triangular system gives $x=3$. The second term is 2, the squared residual that no choice of $x$ can remove. QR obtains the fit without forming $A^TA$, whose two-norm condition number is the square of that of a full-column-rank $A$.\n\n## 4.2 Singular Value Decomposition\n\nFor a real $m\\times n$ matrix, $A=U\\Sigma V^T$ with orthogonal $U,V$ and singular values $\\sigma_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge0$ on the rectangular diagonal. Each right singular vector satisfies $Av_i=\\sigma_i u_i$ for the corresponding paired direction. Squaring the relation gives $A^TAv_i=\\sigma_i^2v_i$. Exactly $r$ positive singular values means rank $r$.\n\n**A weakly observed parameter.** Let $A=\\operatorname{diag}(1,0.001)$. An observation error $(0,0.002)^T$ changes the exact solution by $(0,2)^T$. Both columns are independent, yet the second parameter is a thousand times as sensitive as the first to noise of the same size. Numerical rank therefore depends on a tolerance and the units of the problem.\n\nThe minimum-norm least squares solution is $\\sum_{i=1}^{r}(u_i^Tb/\\sigma_i)v_i$. Components of $b$ outside the image become residual. Components of $x$ in the kernel cannot affect the fit, so the minimum-norm solution sets them to zero. Truncating a small positive singular value deliberately discards sensitivity in that direction at the cost of possible bias.\n\n## 4.3 LU Factorization\n\nGaussian elimination replaces a row by itself minus a multiple of a pivot row. The multipliers form a unit lower triangular matrix $L$ and the final rows form $U$. When rows are exchanged, record their permutation in $P$, giving $PA=LU$. To solve $Ax=b$, apply the same permutation to $b$, then solve $Ly=Pb$ and $Ux=y$.\n\n**Example with a row exchange.** Take $A=\\left[\\begin{smallmatrix}0&2\\\\1&3\\end{smallmatrix}\\right]$ and $b=(4,7)^T$. Swapping the rows gives $U=\\left[\\begin{smallmatrix}1&3\\\\0&2\\end{smallmatrix}\\right]$, $L=I$, and $Pb=(7,4)^T$. The last equation gives $x_2=2$ and the first then gives $x_1=1$. The original leading entry is zero even though the system has a unique solution. A row exchange supplies a usable pivot. With a new load vector and the same matrix, the stored factors remain usable.\n\n## 4.4 Cholesky Factorization\n\nFor symmetric positive definite $H$, elimination gives $H=LDL^T$ with unit lower triangular $L$ and positive diagonal $D$. Absorb $\\sqrt D$ into the lower factor to get the Cholesky form $H=CC^T$. Positive pivots allow this factorization without row exchanges.\n\nFor $H=\\left[\\begin{smallmatrix}4&2\\\\2&5\\end{smallmatrix}\\right]$, the first pivot is 4 and the multiplier is $1/2$. The second pivot is the Schur complement $5-2^2/4=4$. Thus\n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\\\1/2&1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}4&0\\\\0&4\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}2&0\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor $Hx=(6,7)^T$, forward substitution in $Cy=(6,7)^T$ gives $y=(3,2)^T$. Back substitution in $C^Tx=y$ gives $(1,1)^T$. If $H$ is only positive semidefinite, a symmetrically permuted $LDL^T$ can contain zero pivots. For $H=A^TA$, the count of positive diagonal entries of $D$ gives the exact rank of $A$; a singular factor cannot support a unique triangular solve.\n"}, {"title": "Chapter 5. Probability, Estimation, and the Kalman Filter", "path": "courses/robotics-math/ch5.html", "tags": ["Preliminary Mathematics for Robotics", "Mathematics for Robotics"], "summary": "Derive uncertainty-weighted estimates from a sensor model, then predict and update a moving state.", "body": "# Chapter 5. Probability, Estimation, and the Kalman Filter\n\nA distance estimate of 3 m and a new sensor reading of 5 m disagree. How far should the estimate move? The answer depends on the uncertainty of each quantity and on the assumptions that relate them. This chapter builds from a fixed unknown through a random prior to a moving state.\n\n## 5.1 Measurement Error and Estimation\n\nIn a probability model $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$, an outcome describes one possible realization, events collect outcomes, and the probability measure assigns their probabilities. A random variable maps an outcome to a measurable quantity such as a sensor error. Repeating a measurement gives new realizations of that variable.\n\nIn $y=x+v$ with fixed $x$, a zero mean for $v$ means the sensor is unbiased. A bias of 0.2 m persists when independent readings are averaged. With independent zero-mean errors of equal variance $R$, the average of $n$ readings instead has variance $R/n$. Independence enters through the vanishing cross terms in the variance of the sum.\n\n## 5.2 Random Variables and Densities\n\nA scalar density $f$ integrates to one. The probability of an interval is its integral over that interval. The mean is $\\mu=E[X]$ and the variance is $E[(X-\\mu)^2]$. For a vector, $P=E[(X-\\mu)(X-\\mu)^T]$ contains component variances on its diagonal and cross-covariances elsewhere.\n\nCovariance is positive semidefinite because $a^TPa$ is the variance of $a^TX$ for every direction $a$. If two sensors share the same additive bias, their errors can move together. Stacking the sensor readings into a vector preserves that dependence in its off-diagonal entries.\n\n## 5.3 Estimators\n\n**A fixed unknown.** For $y=Cx+v$ with $E[v]=0$, covariance $R\\succ0$, and full-column-rank $C$, a linear estimate $Ky$ is unbiased for every $x$ exactly when $KC=I$. The best linear unbiased estimator (BLUE) is\n\n$$\n\\hat x=(C^TR^{-1}C)^{-1}C^TR^{-1}y,\\qquad\nP_e=(C^TR^{-1}C)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nThis is weighted least squares with inverse covariance as the weight. For independent readings 2 and 4 with variances 1 and 4, respectively, $\\hat x=2.4$ with variance $1/(1+1/4)=0.8$. Gaussianity is unnecessary for this linear optimality result.\n\n**A random unknown.** If the state has prior mean $m$ and covariance $P$ and the noise is uncorrelated with it, the best affine estimate is $m+K(y-Cm)$ with $K=PC^T(CPC^T+R)^{-1}$ when the inverse exists. The estimate can now use information about $x$ before observing $y$. Joint Gaussianity further identifies this affine estimate with the conditional mean, which minimizes mean squared error among all estimators.\n\n## 5.4 Marginal and Conditional Distributions\n\nFor a joint density $f_{X,Y}$, integrate out $y$ to obtain $f_X(x)$. Conditioning fixes the observed value instead: $f_{X|Y}(x|y)=f_{X,Y}(x,y)/f_Y(y)$ wherever $f_Y(y)>0$. Bayes' rule follows by factoring the same joint density in the opposite order.\n\nIndependence gives $f_{X,Y}=f_X f_Y$ when densities exist. With finite second moments it implies zero cross-covariance. The converse can fail: if $X$ is uniform on $[-1,1]$ and $Y=X^2$, symmetry gives $E[XY]=E[X^3]=0$ and $E[X]=0$, yet knowing $X$ fixes $Y$ exactly.\n\n## 5.5 Gaussian Random Vectors\n\nA vector is jointly Gaussian if every linear combination of its components is Gaussian, allowing zero variance. For $X\\sim\\mathcal N(m,P)$, $AX+b$ is Gaussian with mean $Am+b$ and covariance $APA^T$. Selecting rows of the identity gives marginal Gaussian distributions. The transformed covariance can be singular when the outputs satisfy an exact linear relation.\n\nA jointly Gaussian pair with zero cross-covariance is independent. Knowing that two individual marginals are Gaussian does not by itself establish the joint Gaussian assumption.\n\n## 5.6 Conditioning Gaussian Random Vectors\n\nWrite the joint mean of state $X$ and measurement $Y$ as $(m_X,m_Y)$ and their covariance blocks as $P_{XX},P_{XY},P_{YX},P_{YY}$. For a jointly Gaussian pair with invertible $P_{YY}$,\n\n$$\nm_{X|y}=m_X+P_{XY}P_{YY}^{-1}(y-m_Y),\\qquad\nP_{X|y}=P_{XX}-P_{XY}P_{YY}^{-1}P_{YX}.\n$$\n\nTo derive this, subtract $P_{XY}P_{YY}^{-1}(Y-m_Y)$ from $X-m_X$. The remainder has zero covariance with $Y$. Joint Gaussianity makes it independent of $Y$, so its conditional mean is zero and its covariance is the Schur complement above. The measured value shifts the mean; the covariance reduction is determined by the model.\n\n## 5.7 The Discrete-Time Kalman Filter\n\nTake $x_k=Ax_{k-1}+Bu_{k-1}+w_{k-1}$ and $y_k=Cx_k+v_k$, with known input. Assume a Gaussian initial state and mutually independent zero-mean Gaussian process and measurement noises, independent across time and of the initial state. Here $Q$ is process covariance and $R\\succ0$ is measurement covariance. Start from the initial mean and covariance.\n\n| Step | Calculation |\n|---|---|\n| Predict from the previous posterior | $m^-=Am_{k-1}+Bu_{k-1}$ and $P^-=AP_{k-1}A^T+Q$ |\n| Compare prediction with observation | Innovation $d=y_k-Cm^-$, covariance $S=CP^-C^T+R$ |\n| Condition on the observation | $K=P^-C^TS^{-1}$, $m_k=m^-+Kd$, $P_k=P^--KSK^T$ |\n\nThe recursion works because the prediction and measurement are jointly Gaussian given past data. Their cross-covariance is $P^-C^T$. Conditioning therefore gives exactly the update from the preceding section. Feeding the posterior into the next prediction carries the effect of earlier measurements forward.\n\n**One update by hand.** A predicted position of 3 m has variance 4 m². An independent direct sensor reports 5 m with noise variance 1 m². Then $S=5$, $K=4/5$, $m_k=4.6$ m and $P_k=0.8$ m². A sensor with variance 16 m² would give $K=1/5$, moving the estimate only to 3.4 m.\n\n**Nonlinear extension.** An extended Kalman filter propagates the mean through nonlinear dynamics and uses Jacobians of the dynamics and measurement at their respective linearization points to propagate covariance and compute the gain. The resulting posterior is an approximation.\n\n## 5.8 The Luenberger Observer (Optional)\n\nFor a noiseless linear model, consider $\\hat x_{k+1}=A\\hat x_k+Bu_k+L(y_k-C\\hat x_k)$. Subtracting from the true dynamics gives $e_{k+1}=(A-LC)e_k$. In discrete time the error decays for every initial error exactly when all eigenvalues of $A-LC$ lie strictly inside the unit circle.\n\nFor an $n$-state model the observability matrix stacks $C,CA,\\dots,CA^{n-1}$. Full column rank means the initial state is uniquely recoverable from the noiseless outputs with known inputs, and permits arbitrary observer pole placement. The Kalman gain instead follows from the assumed noise and prior covariances.\n\n## 5.9 The Information Matrix (Optional)\n\nFor nonsingular Gaussian covariance, define precision $\\Lambda=P^{-1}$ and information vector $\\eta=\\Lambda m$. The quadratic term of the log density is $-x^T\\Lambda x/2+\\eta^Tx$. An independent linear Gaussian observation adds its log likelihood, giving\n\n$$\n\\Lambda^+=\\Lambda^-+C^TR^{-1}C,\\qquad\n\\eta^+=\\eta^-+C^TR^{-1}y .\n$$\n\nSolve $\\Lambda^+m^+=\\eta^+$ to recover the mean. In the scalar example the prior contributes precision $1/4$ and information $3/4$. The measurement contributes 1 and 5. Thus $m^+=(3/4+5)/(1/4+1)=4.6$, the same result as the covariance update.\n\n## 5.10 Deriving the Minimum Variance Estimator (Optional)\n\nFor centered variables, minimize the error covariance of an estimate $Ky$. Put $S=CPC^T+R$ and $K_*=PC^TS^{-1}$. Expanding the error and completing the square gives\n\n$$\nP_e(K)=P-PC^TS^{-1}CP+(K-K_*)S(K-K_*)^T .\n$$\n\nThe last term is positive semidefinite for every $K$, establishing optimality in every direction. Equivalently, the optimal error is orthogonal in expectation to the observation: $E[(x-K_*y)y^T]=0$. This is the probabilistic counterpart of the residual orthogonality used in [deterministic least squares](ch3.md). For nonzero means, center the variables before the calculation and add the prior mean back afterward.\n"}, {"title": "Chapter 6. Real Analysis", "path": "courses/robotics-math/ch6.html", "tags": ["Preliminary Mathematics for Robotics", "Mathematics for Robotics"], "summary": "Distinguish small steps from convergence and use completeness, contraction, and compactness to prove guarantees.", "body": "# Chapter 6. Real Analysis\n\nAn iterative solver produces a sequence of estimates. Small updates can be encouraging even when the estimates drift without bound. What evidence proves that they approach a point, that the point is feasible, and that it solves the equation?\n\n## 6.1 Open and Closed Sets in Normed Spaces\n\nIn a normed space the open ball $B_r(x)$ contains all $y$ with $\\|y-x\\|<r$. A point is interior to $S$ when some ball about it lies inside $S$. It is a boundary point if every ball meets both $S$ and its complement. A set is open when all its points are interior and closed when it contains its closure.\n\nDistance to a nonempty set is $d(x,S)=\\inf_{s\\in S}\\|x-s\\|$. The condition $d(x,S)=0$ identifies the closure. For allowable travel $S=(0,1]$, the endpoint 0 has distance zero but is infeasible. The boundary is $\\{0,1\\}$, the interior is $(0,1)$, and the closure is $[0,1]$. Replacing a strict constraint by a closed one changes whether a limiting configuration is allowable.\n\n## 6.2 Newton’s Method\n\nTo solve $F(x)=0$, approximate $F(x_k+s)$ by $F(x_k)+J_F(x_k)s$. Solve $J_F(x_k)s_k=-F(x_k)$ and set $x_{k+1}=x_k+s_k$. For a square system, invertibility of the Jacobian gives a unique linearized step. Local quadratic convergence holds near a simple root under suitable smoothness, for example a locally Lipschitz Jacobian and a sufficiently close initial estimate.\n\nFor $F(x)=x^2-2$, the update is $x_{k+1}=(x_k+2/x_k)/2$. Starting at 1 gives $1.5$ and then $17/12$. With $r=\\sqrt2$, the exact error satisfies $x_{k+1}-r=(x_k-r)^2/(2x_k)$, which explains the rapid decrease near $r$. At zero the linearized step is undefined.\n\n## 6.3 Sequences and Convergence\n\nConvergence $x_k\\to x$ means that for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is an index $N$ after which *all* terms satisfy $\\|x_k-x\\|<\\varepsilon$. A limit is unique: if two proposed limits were distance $d>0$ apart, sufficiently late iterates would be within $d/3$ of both, contradicting the triangle inequality.\n\nA point lies in the closure of $S$ exactly when some sequence from $S$ converges to it. To construct such a sequence at a closure point, choose $x_k$ in the intersection of $S$ and the ball of radius $1/k$. An isolated point of $S$ has a neighborhood containing no other member, so a sequence in $S$ converging to that point is eventually constant.\n\n## 6.4 Cauchy Sequences and Completeness\n\nA sequence is Cauchy when for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is an $N$ such that **every pair** of terms beyond $N$ is within $\\varepsilon$. Convergence implies this by bounding each term’s distance from the limit by $\\varepsilon/2$. The condition $\\|x_{k+1}-x_k\\|\\to0$ alone is weaker: the partial sums of $1/k$ keep growing although their steps vanish.\n\nA space is complete if every Cauchy sequence has a limit in that space. Rational decimal approximations to $\\sqrt 2$ are Cauchy in the usual metric but have no rational limit. Euclidean space is complete, as is any closed subset with its inherited distance. Completeness can depend on the norm: continuous functions on a compact interval are complete in the uniform norm; under the integral one-norm, continuous ramps can approach a discontinuous step in integral error.\n\n## 6.5 The Contraction Mapping Theorem\n\nSuppose $T:S\\to S$ on a nonempty complete set and $\\|T(x)-T(y)\\|\\le c\\|x-y\\|$ for one constant $0\\le c<1$ throughout $S$. For $x_{k+1}=T(x_k)$ the successive differences shrink geometrically. Summing their tail gives the usable error bound\n\n$$\n\\|x_m-x_k\\|\\le\\frac{c^k}{1-c}\\|x_1-x_0\\|,\\qquad m>k .\n$$\n\nThe sequence is Cauchy, so it converges in $S$. Continuity of $T$ makes the limit a fixed point. If two points were fixed, their distance would be at most $c$ times itself, forcing equality of the points.\n\nFor $T(x)=(x+2)/3$ on $[0,2]$, the interval maps into itself and $c=1/3$. Iterating from 0 gives $2/3,8/9,\\ldots$, with fixed point 1. A stopping certificate follows from the same geometric sum: the remaining error at $x_k$ is at most $\\|x_{k+1}-x_k\\|/(1-c)$.\n\n## 6.6 Continuous Functions\n\nContinuity at $x$ means that every output tolerance $\\varepsilon>0$ admits an input radius $\\delta>0$ such that all domain points within that radius have outputs within $\\varepsilon$ of $f(x)$. In normed spaces this is equivalent to preserving the limit of every sequence converging to $x$.\n\nTo disprove continuity it suffices to find one sequence. A threshold sensor reporting 1 for $x\\ge0$ and 0 otherwise jumps at zero: inputs $-1/k$ approach zero but their outputs remain 0, whereas $f(0)=1$. For a positive result, the reverse triangle inequality $|\\|x\\|-\\|y\\||\\le\\|x-y\\|$ proves continuity of the norm by taking $\\delta=\\varepsilon$.\n\n## 6.7 Compact Sets and Extrema\n\nIn a metric space compactness is equivalent to every sequence having a subsequence that converges to a point in the set. In finite-dimensional normed spaces, closedness and boundedness together characterize compactness. All norms in a finite-dimensional space are equivalent, so they give the same convergent sequences.\n\n**Why an optimum is attained.** Let $f$ be continuous on a nonempty compact feasible set. Its image is compact in $\\mathbb R$, hence bounded. Take feasible points whose costs approach the infimum. Compactness gives a subsequence converging to a feasible point, and continuity makes the cost there equal to the infimum. Applying the same reasoning to $-f$ yields a maximum.\n\nFor $f(x)=x^2$ on $(0,1]$, points $1/k$ drive the cost to zero but the optimizer is missing. On $[0,1]$ the same cost attains its minimum at zero. [Convexity](ch7.md) addresses a different question: once a local minimizer exists, when must it be global?\n"}, {"title": "Chapter 7. Introduction to Optimization", "path": "courses/robotics-math/ch7.html", "tags": ["Preliminary Mathematics for Robotics", "Mathematics for Robotics"], "summary": "Compare three calibration fits and formulate their objectives as quadratic or linear programs.", "body": "# Chapter 7. Introduction to Optimization\n\nThree calibration readings for a single offset are 0, 0, and 6. Which offset fits them best? Squared error, total absolute error, and worst-case error give three different answers. Each criterion expresses a different engineering requirement, and each can be written as a convex optimization problem.\n\n## 7.1 Convex Sets and Convex Functions\n\nA feasible set $D$ is convex if $(1-t)x+ty$ remains in $D$ for all $x,y\\in D$ and $0\\le t\\le1$. A convex combination uses nonnegative weights summing to one. All finite convex combinations of a set form its **convex hull**, the smallest convex set containing it. Affine equality constraints and linear inequalities define convex sets, and their intersection remains convex.\n\nA function on a convex domain is convex if\n$$\nf((1-t)x+ty)\\le(1-t)f(x)+tf(y).\n$$\n\n**Why local optimality suffices.** Suppose $x$ is locally minimizing but a feasible $y$ has lower cost. For every $0<t\\le1$ the segment point is feasible and has cost strictly below $f(x)$. Choosing $t$ arbitrarily small places these better points inside any neighborhood of $x$, a contradiction. Strict convexity further gives at most one minimizer. Existence still needs its own argument: $f(x)=x$ on $(0,1)$ is convex and has no minimizer.\n\n## 7.2 Optimization Notation\n\nFor a nonempty feasible set $D$, $\\inf_{x\\in D} f(x)$ denotes the greatest lower bound of achievable costs. When attained, it equals the minimum. The notation $\\arg\\min_{x\\in D}f(x)$ denotes the set of feasible points attaining that value. Writing $x^*\\in\\arg\\min f$ allows for several optimal decisions.\n\nFor example, minimize $(u_1+u_2-3)^2$ subject to $u_1,u_2\\ge0$. The minimum cost is zero. The minimizers form the segment from $(3,0)$ to $(0,3)$, representing all nonnegative allocations giving the same total command. A solver can return one point from the segment while the model leaves the split undetermined.\n\n## 7.3 Quadratic Programs\n\nA quadratic program minimizes $\\tfrac12 x^THx+g^Tx$ subject to affine equalities, componentwise inequalities, and possibly bounds on each variable. A symmetric $H\\succeq0$ makes the objective convex. If $H\\succ0$, any attained minimum on a convex feasible set is unique.\n\n**From a residual to a QP.** Expanding $\\tfrac12\\|Ax-b\\|_2^2$ gives $H=A^TA$ and $g=-A^Tb$. The omitted constant $\\tfrac12 b^Tb$ affects the reported cost but leaves the optimizing decision unchanged. For a weight matrix $W\\succ0$, replace these coefficients by $A^TWA$ and $-A^TWb$.\n\nFor the three readings, the unscaled squared error is $2x^2+(x-6)^2=3(x-2)^2+24$. The optimal offset is 2 and the minimum squared error is 24. If calibration limits require $0\\le x\\le1$, the closest feasible point to 2 is 1, with cost 27. This scalar example also shows why solving the unconstrained normal equations alone can produce an infeasible decision.\n\n## 7.4 Linear Programs and Norm Minimization\n\nA linear program has a linear objective and affine constraints. Absolute values become linear inequalities by adding variables that bound the residuals. With $r=Ax-b$, the two useful formulations are\n\n| Objective | Equivalent linear program |\n|---|---|\n| Total absolute error $\\|r\\|_1$ | Minimize $\\sum_i s_i$ subject to $-s_i\\le r_i\\le s_i$ for each $i$. |\n| Worst-case error $\\|r\\|_\\infty$ | Minimize $t$ subject to $-t\\le r_i\\le t$ for each $i$. |\n\nFor fixed $x$, the smallest feasible $s_i$ equals $|r_i|$, and the smallest shared $t$ equals $\\max_i |r_i|$. This proves equivalence and explains why the first formulation needs one auxiliary variable per residual while the second needs only one in total. Additional affine constraints on $x$ can be carried into either formulation.\n\n**Compare the three fits.** For the unconstrained offset data $0,0,6$, total absolute error is $2|x|+|x-6|$. On $[0,6]$ it equals $6+x$ and outside this interval it exceeds 6. The unique minimizer is 0, the median. For worst-case error, $|x|$ and $|6-x|$ cannot both be below 3 because their sum is at least 6. At $x=3$ both equal 3, attaining this lower bound.\n\n| Fit criterion | Offset | Engineering interpretation |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Sum of squared errors | 2 | The large residual pulls the estimate toward the distant reading. |\n| Sum of absolute errors | 0 | The two agreeing readings determine the median. |\n| Maximum absolute error | 3 | The offset balances the errors at the two extremes. |\n\nThe observations and model are identical in all three fits. The requirement on residuals determines which estimate is optimal.\n"}];